<http://data.silknow.org/object/3e72ca55-1205-3b89-91c1-5aff7fcfcc61>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Furnishing fabric sample of woven silk and wool double cloth in 8 colourways."@en , "Charles Voysey was one of the most original and influential architects and designers of all forms of decorative art working at the end of the 19th century. In 1882 he set up his own architecture practice and from the late 1880s started to design repeating patterns for wallpaper, woven and printed textiles and carpets. \n\nVoysey's most interesting designs date from this time and show the influence of William Morris's principles of pattern design and use of plant forms and animals as standard motifs. Voysey's textiles are dominated by flowing patterns incorporating birds, deer, hearts, flowers and trees in silhouette. He sold his work to manufacturers such as G. P. & J. Baker, Thomas Wardle and Alexander Morton, and many of his designs were sold through Liberty in London. Voysey's work was well known in continental Europe, and though popular with French Art Nouveau designers, his influence was felt more dramatically by the founders of the Modern movement."@en , "Furnishing fabric sample 'Rowalan' or 'Seagulls' of woven silk and wool double cloth, designed by C. F. A. Voysey for Alexander Morton & Co., Great Britain, 1893."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/c1a3cf77-a894-35b6-b70a-d678b781d9b3> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Furnishing fabric sample of woven silk and wool double cloth in 8 colourways."@en , "Furnishing fabric sample 'Rowalan' or 'Seagulls' of woven silk and wool double cloth, designed by C. F. A. Voysey for Alexander Morton & Co., Great Britain, 1893."@en , "Charles Voysey was one of the most original and influential architects and designers of all forms of decorative art working at the end of the 19th century. In 1882 he set up his own architecture practice and from the late 1880s started to design repeating patterns for wallpaper, woven and printed textiles and carpets. \n\nVoysey's most interesting designs date from this time and show the influence of William Morris's principles of pattern design and use of plant forms and animals as standard motifs. Voysey's textiles are dominated by flowing patterns incorporating birds, deer, hearts, flowers and trees in silhouette. He sold his work to manufacturers such as G. P. & J. Baker, Thomas Wardle and Alexander Morton, and many of his designs were sold through Liberty in London. Voysey's work was well known in continental Europe, and though popular with French Art Nouveau designers, his influence was felt more dramatically by the founders of the Modern movement."@en ;
        ecrm:P43_has_dimension         <http://data.silknow.org/object/3e72ca55-1205-3b89-91c1-5aff7fcfcc61/dimension/2> , <http://data.silknow.org/object/3e72ca55-1205-3b89-91c1-5aff7fcfcc61/dimension/1> ;
        dc:identifier                  "CIRC.94-1966" ;
        <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs>
                <https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O287720> .

